  "deprecation": {
    "@id": "https://api.nuget.org/v3/catalog0/data/2026.07.28.11.06.30/qrshard.core.1.3.9.json#deprecation",
    "message": "Security: 1.3.9 fixed the decode path traversal (CWE-22) only for single-file payloads. Decoding an ARCHIVE payload - a folder, carried as a tar - still built its destination directory from the attacker-controlled header name, so a crafted image could write into the parent of the working directory, overwriting files. GetFileNameWithoutExtension is not a sanitizer: a name of \"...\" becomes \"..\".\nIf you upgraded to 1.3.9 for the earlier advisory, upgrade again. Fixed in 1.3.10, the first release where both the single-file and archive paths are safe.\nDecodeImages(paths) is affected because outputPath defaults to null. Workaround if you cannot upgrade yet: always pass outputPath explicitly - it is used exactly as given and is not influenced by the shard header.\nDetails: https://github.com/lfarrand/QrShard/releases/tag/v1.3.10",
    "reasons": [
      "Other",
      "CriticalBugs"
    ]
  },
  "description": "Codec library for QrShard: encode any file into dense QR-style images and decode captures (screenshots, photos, recordings) back into the original, bit-for-bit (SHA-256 verified). Public API: QrShardCodec.",
